5
00:00:10.364 --> 00:00:11.260
Problem.

6
00:00:12.510 --> 00:00:15.369
Two replicas each hold 100,000 keys.

7
00:00:15.649 --> 00:00:17.035
A few writes were missed.

8
00:00:17.315 --> 00:00:21.838
Find exactly which keys differ while exchanging as few hashes as possible.

9
00:00:22.118 --> 00:00:26.363
Sending all the data, or even all the per-key hashes, is too expensive.

10
00:00:27.713 --> 00:00:28.588
Part 2.

11
00:00:28.868 --> 00:00:29.785
Key idea.

12
00:00:31.035 --> 00:00:35.494
Split the key space into buckets and hash each bucket's contents (the leaves).

13
00:00:35.774 --> 00:00:37.843
Each parent is hash(left ‖ right).

14
00:00:38.123 --> 00:00:40.897
If the roots match, the replicas are identical.

15
00:00:41.177 --> 00:00:44.675
Otherwise descend only into subtrees whose hashes differ.

16
00:00:44.955 --> 00:00:49.649
With d differences in B buckets, that's about O(d, log B) comparisons.

17
00:00:49.929 --> 00:00:54.089
Cassandra repair, DynamoDB, Git and blockchains all use this.

