104
00:07:30.883 --> 00:07:31.758
Part 8.

105
00:07:32.038 --> 00:07:33.147
Common mistakes.

106
00:07:34.397 --> 00:07:37.235
Bucketing by insertion order instead of by key.

107
00:07:37.515 --> 00:07:41.461
The two replicas then bucket differently and everything appears to differ.

108
00:07:41.741 --> 00:07:43.853
Rebuilding the whole tree on every write.

109
00:07:44.133 --> 00:07:49.616
Update the leaf and its ancestors incrementally, or rebuild periodically for repair runs.

110
00:07:49.896 --> 00:07:53.821
Too few buckets, so each differing bucket still means shipping many keys.

111
00:07:54.101 --> 00:07:55.829
Too many buckets inflate the tree.

112
00:07:56.109 --> 00:07:58.371
Tune B to the expected divergence.

113
00:07:59.721 --> 00:08:00.617
Part 9.

114
00:08:00.897 --> 00:08:02.241
Follow-up questions.

115
00:08:03.491 --> 00:08:04.365
Question.

116
00:08:04.645 --> 00:08:06.267
How does Cassandra use this?

117
00:08:06.547 --> 00:08:16.168
Thirty-second answer: nodetool repair builds Merkle trees per token range on each replica, exchanges them, and streams only the differing ranges.

118
00:08:16.448 --> 00:08:21.589
Incremental repair marks repaired SSTables so later runs cover only new data.

119
00:08:22.939 --> 00:08:23.814
Question.

120
00:08:24.094 --> 00:08:27.486
Merkle trees versus read repair versus hinted handoff?

121
00:08:27.766 --> 00:08:29.131
Thirty-second answer:

122
00:08:29.411 --> 00:08:33.017
Hinted handoff replays writes missed during short outages.

123
00:08:33.297 --> 00:08:35.814
Read repair fixes the keys you happen to read.

124
00:08:36.094 --> 00:08:41.833
Merkle-tree anti-entropy finds all divergence, including cold keys nobody reads.

125
00:08:42.113 --> 00:08:43.969
You need all three for convergence.

126
